fair-share distortion, not a corner.

## What was asked

That handoff flagged one consequence as analytically implied but explicitly not asserted:

> **Analytically implied, NOT measured — flagging as unverified.** [...] the claim is also the model's
> **ranking and entitlement quantity** in a multi-model pass, so an inflated claim should win a larger
> share against other models with no second analyzer involved. [...] it is the question that decides
> whether this is a corner or a fair-share distortion.

It is cheap on the harness I already had, so I measured it rather than leaving it open. **It
reproduces.**

## Fixture

Two models contend for a **4-GPU** pool. Both are **sat-only** — one analyzer, so there is no `W4`
escape anywhere in this fixture. Both models truly need **3 GPUs**: demand 30000 at PRC 10000, served
at 1 GPU/replica.

Model **X** additionally owns a reference variant it **cannot buy** — `cheap-x`, pinned at
`MaxReplicas 1` with `CurrentReplicas 1`, so headroom is zero and the picker can never land on it.
It is nonetheless the cost-efficiency winner (Cost 5 vs 20 at equal PRC), so
`referenceVariantForRole` selects it and `claimGPUs` prices X's whole claim through it.

Model **Y** has a single variant, `y-v`, at 1 GPU/replica.

**The only difference between the two runs is `cheap-x`'s `GPUsPerReplica`.** Nothing about what
either model needs, nothing about what either model is able to buy, and nothing about the pool.

## Result

| `cheap-x` GPUs/replica | X gets | Y gets | total |
|---|---|---|---|
| **3** | `pricey-x` **4** (+3) | `y-v` **2** (+1) | 4 GPUs |
| **1** | `pricey-x` **3** (+2) | `y-v` **3** (+2) | 4 GPUs |

With the asymmetry, X's claim prices at `30000/10000 × 3 = 9` GPUs against a true need of 3, and X
takes 3 of the 4 pool GPUs. Without it, the claim is honest at 3 and the pool splits evenly.

**One GPU moves from Y to X because a variant X cannot buy is described as using three GPUs per
replica.** Y is a bystander — it has no unbuyable variant, no asymmetry, and no second analyzer.

Two things worth noting for your read:

- **The pool is honored in both runs** (4 = 4). This is purely a redistribution, which is why no
  pool-based safety net catches it and why no existing golden would.
- **`n = min(bottleneck, capN)` does not protect here.** It bounds what X spends *within* one model,
  which is why the single-analyzer single-model case looks fine. It says nothing about how large a
  share X was granted relative to Y, and the grant is what moved.

This makes the `GPUsPerReplica` mismatch reachable **without** the `W4` escape and **without** TA.
Your framing — that `W4`'s escape reveals the inflation rather than causing it — is what the numbers
support: here the inflation is visible with the escape absent entirely.

## Scope note

I am reporting a measurement, not proposing a fix. The claim-pricing question is Type-1 and yours;
`referenceVariantForRole` and `claimGPUs` are both inside the function C6e just rewrote, and per
§7.1 L2257-2260 the conversion is a decided `W`-item mechanism I am not re-deciding.

Also confirming your correction: my option 2b was wrong. The clamp at `allocateForModel:440-469` runs
before the pick at `:471+`, so there is no picked variant to key on. Disregard it.

